WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:08.800
If you love flying as much as I do.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.750
Your priority will always be getting that
window seat.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:15.620
Some of my favourite experiences from travelling
have been experienced through the window of

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:16.620
a plane.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.980
From looking down on the Burj Khalifa to returning
to the green fields of Ireland.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.390
Flying has always been a special experience
for me.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.980
So when I got a chance to fly on the Boeing
787 Dreamliner, with it’s huge windows I

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.869
was extremely excited.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:34.350
I had been waiting for years to get the opportunity
to fly in this ground breaking plane.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:39.060
To some of you it may just look like another
everyday passenger plane, but the 787 is a

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.760
revolutionary plane.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:45.089
The dreamliner was the first commercial passenger
plane to fly with the majority of its structure

00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:49.350
being built with composite materials, mostly
carbon reinforced plastics.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.030
This reduced the weight of the aircraft dramatically,
helping it to be around 20% more fuel efficient

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.120
than similar sized aircraft.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.910
That 20% increase in fuel efficiency isn’t
just saving you and the airline money, but

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.960
it has allowed new flight routes, that were
previously financially unviable to open.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.190
You see, the 787 is not a huge plane.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.920
It can carry less than half the passengers
than the double decker airbus A380 and fills

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.690
the size gap between the Long-Range Boeing
777 and short to mid range 737.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.979
Boeing specifically built this plane to serve
more niche routes that larger planes could

00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:27.170
not serve, as the amount of people flying
between the locations was too low to sell

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.090
the number of tickets needed to make it profitable.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.080
Airlines call these long, thin routes.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.370
Want to fly from Dublin to Los Angeles directly?

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.260
The 787 made that possible.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.390
No more stopping in London or New York.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:43.710
This ability may have even saved Polish airlines
from bankruptcy, as the dreamliner finally

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:49.299
allowed them to profitably fly directly from
their Warsaw hub to New York, Chicago and

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:50.299
Toronto.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:54.080
This is great for airlines and great for any
of us who live in relatively small cities

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.360
that usually have to travel to the nearest
large airport to fly to our final destination.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:04.659
But my favourite thing about the Boeing 787
are those huge windows.The 787s windows are

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:07.320
this much larger than an average airplane
window.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.951
If you are anything like me you always end
up with your face crammed next to the window

00:02:10.951 --> 00:02:15.790
trying to get the best view, but with the
787 you can see so much while sitting back

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:16.819
and relaxing.

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:19.870
It really gives your this wonderful panoramic
view feeling.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:24.120
It also has these electronic dimmers, which
do away with the need for those crappy plastic

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.080
shutters, which people insist on opening while
others are trying to sleep.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.050
That isn’t possible on the 787 as the master
dimming switch is controlled by the flight

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.050
attendants.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:37.540
So you may be wondering, why are these windows
so much larger than other planes?

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.120
This is another advantage of composite materials.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.450
You may remember from my first video, early
passenger planes like the De Havilland Comet

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.340
had a series of unfortunate midair explosions,
due to stress concentration at the corners

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.340
of their square windows.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.709
After intensive research the investigators
discovered that the square windows were blocking

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:56.270
the flow of stress in the structure, making
it pile up in the corners of the window.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:00.060
Round windows allowed the stress to flow much
more smoothly around the windows and thus

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.530
our windows have been rounded ever since,
but shape is not the only thing that can elevate

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.890
stress concentrations.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.122
The size of the window has a huge effect on
it too.

00:03:08.122 --> 00:03:13.360
A larger window means more stress has to detour
around the window and thus the stress concentration

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.379
is elevated, which can result in cracks and
failures over repeated cabin pressurisation

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:17.379
cycles.

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:20.340
So these large windows do result in higher
levels of stress concentration around the

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.750
787 windows.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:25.989
But I failed to mention one factor in that
short little 2 minute video a year ago.

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:26.989
Fatigue.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:30.830
Over the course of a plane’s life it is
repeatedly pressurised and unpressurised as

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.220
it rises and falls through the atmosphere.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.200
This means the material experiences varying
stresses continually over it’s life that

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.250
can damage it.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.140
This was the second piece of the puzzle for
the De Havilland Comet mystery and it’s

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.620
engineers proved it by building this huge
water tank where pressurisation test could

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:52.860
be done quickly . The test fuselage had undergone
1,230 normal flights and 1,830 tank flights

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.780
before the fuselage failed at the corner of
the square windows.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.850
This is why parts need to be replaced after
years of use.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:03.340
A part may handle a single application of
a load without any problem, but after many

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.590
of cycles of loading the material will begin
to weaken as tiny cracks form in the metallic

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:08.590
structure.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:12.579
Metals like aluminium, which is traditionally
used for aircraft fuselage, have a natural

00:04:12.579 --> 00:04:14.700
tendency to form cracks over time.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.090
This is because small defects in the crystal
structure called dislocations, which are essentially

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:23.750
holes in the atomic structure create weakness
that makes it easier to deform.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.040
These dislocations can travel through the
metal structure and have a tendency to coalesce,and

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:32.820
so after hundreds of cycles what was once
a small defect can grow into a large defect

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.750
in the form of a crack and these cracks cause
failure of the part.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:40.440
Fatigue failure in composite materials is
incredible complex and we still have a lot

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.770
to learn about the subject.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.070
I have no problem admitting right now, I don’t
understand it enough to teach any of you with

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:50.350
confidence on the details and I have a thesis
specialising in the subject.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.940
But I can say this with confidence, fatigue
failure does not happen as quickly in carbon

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.669
fibre reinforced fuselages and there are many
tests to prove that.

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:02.600
The Boeing 787 fuselage is manufactured by
wrapping layer after layer of resin soaked

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.919
carbon fibre around the fuselage many times.

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:09.970
This creates a structure that is very resistant
to fatigue as the high strength fibres are

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.710
wrapped continuously around the fuselage.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:16.710
Fatigue failure will eventually occur in the
plastic resin holding the fibres together,

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:19.340
but it takes a great deal longer than in aluminium.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:24.009
This allows the carbon reinforced plastic
frame of 787 to tolerate higher stresses in

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:26.240
the fuselage and last longer doing it.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.550
There is still a lot to be learned about the
mechanics of composite materials.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:34.160
Composite materials are a difficult material
to work with at the best of times and we will

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.330
be learning more about that in the next video,
where we will be learning more about the history

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:42.780
of the material and I will be visiting a local
composites factory to show you more about

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.720
the manufacturing process.

